8. Write the TestNG Program  
Copy the following code:  
package tests;  
  
import org.openqa.selenium.By; import 
org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;  
import 
org.openqa.selenium.firefox.FirefoxDriver;  
  
import org.testng.Assert;  import org.testng.annotations.AfterMethod; import org.testng.annotations.BeforeMethod; import org.testng.annotations.Test;  
  
public class SauceDemoTest {  
  
    WebDriver driver;  
  
    @BeforeMethod     public void setUp() {  
  
        driver = new FirefoxDriver();          driver.manage().window().maximize();  
  
        driver.get("https://www.saucedemo.com/");     }  
  
    // Test Case 1  
    @Test(priority = 1, groups = {"Smoke"})     public void loginTest() {           driver.findElement(By.id("username"))  
              .sendKeys("standard_user");  
         driver.findElement(By.id("password"))  
              .sendKeys("secret_sauce");  
  
        driver.findElement(By.id("loginbutton"))  
              .click();  
  
        String url = driver.getCurrentUrl();   
        Assert.assertTrue(url.contains("inventory"));  
  
        System.out.println("Login Test Passed");  
    }  
  
    // Test Case 2  
    @Test(priority = 2, groups = {"Smoke"})     public void titleTest() {  
  
        String title = driver.getTitle();  
  
        Assert.assertEquals(title, "Swag 
Labs");  
  
        System.out.println("Title Test 
Passed");     }  
  
    // Test Case 3  
    @Test(priority = 3, groups = {"Regression"})     public void invalidLoginTest() {  
  
        driver.findElement(By.id("user-name"))  
              .sendKeys("wrong_user");           driver.findElement(By.id("password"))  
              .sendKeys("wrong_password");  
  
        driver.findElement(By.id("login-
button"))  
              .click();  
  
        boolean errorMessage =  
                driver.findElement(By.cssSelector("[data-test='error']"))                     
.isDisplayed();  
  
        Assert.assertTrue(errorMessage);  
  
        System.out.println("Invalid Login Test Passed");      }  
  
    @AfterMethod  
    public void tearDown() {  
  
        driver.quit();  
    } } 
   
9. Understand the TestNG Annotations  
@BeforeMethod  
@BeforeMethod public void setUp()  
This method runs before every test case.  
It:  
1.	Opens Firefox.  
2.	Maximizes the browser.  
3.	Opens the website.  
   
@Test Example:  
@Test(priority = 1, groups = {"Smoke"})  
This tells TestNG that the method is a test case.  
  priority We have:  
priority = 1 priority = 2 priority = 3  
Therefore TestNG executes them in this order:  
1	→ Login Test  
2	→ Title Test  
3	→ Invalid Login Test  
Lower priority number executes first.  
   
10. TestNG Groups  
We have created two groups.  
Smoke 	Group 
groups = {"Smoke"} 
Contains:  
Login Test  
Title Test Regression 
Group 	groups 	= 
{"Regression"} Contains:  
Invalid Login Test  
Groups are useful when we want to execute only selected test cases.  
   
11. TestNG Assertion  
For login:  
Assert.assertTrue(url.contains("inventory")); 
It checks whether login was successful.  For title:  
Assert.assertEquals(title, "Swag Labs");  
It checks whether the page title is correct.  
   
